{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NiGwh1oD4Q65kUrVGAz9RE","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/paper_framing.md\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# Paper Framing — irt_equated_bench_regen\\\\n\\\\n## Target Venue\\\\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 (via ARR; long paper 8-page limit + unlimited refs)\\\\n- Page limit: 8 pages main text (当前 10 页，需压缩 2 页)\\\\n\\\\n## Paper Type\\\\nEmpirical analysis / psychometric characterization. Discovery paper, not methods paper. \\\\\\\"DIF reveals benchmark structural problems,\\\\\\\" not \\\\\\\"DIF detects contamination.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n## Core Claims (4 条, 可证伪)\\\\n\\\\n**Claim 1 (Temporal Comparability Concern)**: ~31% of MMLU items are functionally unstable across model generations — they exhibit large DIF (ETS C, |Δ_MH|≥1.5) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts. Primary estimate: **31.3%** (temporal cohort, plan_001). All 57 subjects show instability (C% range 20.7–48.4%, unweighted mean ≈30%, plan_001 domain_specificity.csv). Within-subject matching on 5 high-C% subjects yields higher rates (31-45%, avg 38.1%, plan_014) — report as robustness check, not primary. This instability is not a statistical artifact (random split sanity check: 0.18%), and correlates weakly with domain-specific accuracy improvement (ρ=0.307, p=0.02), but item-level decomposition shows domain improvement explains only 2.4pp (7.6%). Architecture heterogeneity is a non-issue: METABENCH MMLU is 99.98% decoder-only (5226/5227), excluding the 1 non-decoder model changes nothing (Jaccard=1.0). Threshold sensitivity robustness confirmed: C% ranges from 48.9% (|Δ_MH|≥1.0) to 18.3% (|Δ_MH|≥2.0) to 10.2% (|Δ_MH|≥2.5). BH FDR correction at q=0.05 has negligible impact (removes 3/3,918 items).\\\\n- Evidence: plan_001 (C%=31.3%), plan_014 (within-subject C%=38.1%), plan_010 (arch-stratified: 99.98% decoder-only, C% unchanged, Jaccard=1.0), plan_018 (threshold: 49/31/18/10% at 1.0/1.5/2.0/2.5; BH negligible), plan_019 (decomposition: ≤2.4pp/7.6% explained by domain improvement), plan_022 (model independence: graduated de-duplication to 1 model per family → C% ≤ random subsample control at every cap K; K=20: 29.1% vs random 30.9%, K=5: 11.7% vs random 16.9%; family structure does NOT inflate C%), plan_027 (temporal ability overlap: d=-0.17, OVL=76.4%; 1:1 NN matching C%=28.9%±0.1% vs full 31.3% — ability confound explains only 2.4pp; contrast with Llama-2v3 d=-0.99→matched C%=28.3%, where ability confound explains 33pp)\\\\n- Honesty requirement: ρ=0.307 (p=0.02) at subject level reflects a modest ecological correlation. Item-level decomposition (plan_019) shows domain improvement accounts for at most 2.4pp of 31.3% C% (7.6%; pseudo R²=0.0007). The instability is predominantly item-specific. Caveat: item difficulty may be a mediator rather than confounder — use Model 1 as primary; Model 2 (Simpson's paradox) as supplementary.\\\\n- IRT fit: 98% of models show |Lz|>2, reflecting MMLU's inherent multidimensionality (57 subjects), not architecture heterogeneity. 1D IRT is fundamentally misspecified for MMLU — discuss in limitations as motivation for future multidimensional IRT work.\\\\n\\\\n**Claim 2 (Ground Truth Meta-critique)**: Existing contamination detection literature validates against web-overlap/n-gram labels that measure global exposure (whether an item is publicly accessible), not differential contamination (whether an item functions differently across model cohorts). DIF enrichment≈1.0 against Li et al. labels is expected, not failure — these labels cannot evaluate item-level differential detectors. This is a fundamental validation methodology gap: all work using web-overlap labels as ground truth for item-level detectors (including Min-K%, n-gram methods) has the same blind spot. **Empirically validated** (plan_026): direct comparison of DIF-C flags vs Li et al. web-overlap labels on 9,229 MMLU items shows enrichment=0.964, Jaccard=0.206, Phi=0.012, χ²=1.28 (p=0.258), OR=1.05 [0.96, 1.15]. Consistent across subtypes (input_only: 0.986, input_and_label: 0.960). Semi-synthetic injection confirms DIF can detect differential contamination (ΔTPR=+0.508 at p=0.5, dose-response from p=0.3 to p=1.0).\\\\n- Evidence: plan_001 (enrichment=0.96), plan_026 (direct comparison: enrichment=0.964, Jaccard=0.206, p=0.258), plan_011 (ΔTPR=+0.508, dose-response)\\\\n- Framing: aggressive — \\\\\\\"the field's standard validation approach is fundamentally mismatched for item-level detection\\\\\\\" — now with direct empirical evidence, not just conceptual argument\\\\n\\\\n**Claim 3 (Semi-synthetic Validation Framework)**: The ΔTPR methodology — injecting known differential contamination into real response matrices and measuring detection power increase — provides a controlled validation infrastructure for any item-level contamination detector. Cross-subject matching (using total score on OTHER subjects as MH matching variable) eliminates FPR inflation (ΔFPR=0.000 across all 5 tested subjects), while within-subject matching suffers from matching variable contamination (FPR inflation +0.186).\\\\n- Evidence: plan_011, plan_016 (ΔFPR=0.000 cross-subject, 4/5 subjects ΔTPR>0.3), plan_004 Step 2 (GSM8K ΔTPR=+0.818 standard, +0.517 external)\\\\n- Cross-benchmark: GSM8K replication confirms framework portability to single-domain math benchmarks\\\\n- Positioning: cross-subject matching is a practical methodological recommendation; cross-benchmark replication strengthens generalizability\\\\n\\\\n**Claim 4 (Downstream Impact — Four-Layer Finding)**: DIF as diagnostic lens, not quality filter. Four sub-findings:\\\\n1. **Benchmark redundancy**: ρ≈0.997 between full and stable-only rankings — MMLU rankings are extremely robust to 31% item removal, revealing massive measurement redundancy in the benchmark.\\\\n2. **DIF items carry cross-cohort signal but are NOT the most ranking-defining items**: DIF removal causes MORE disruption than random removal (z=-8 to -49σ, 21/21 conditions), confirming DIF items are non-trivial. But circularity ablation (plan_020) shows DIF is the LEAST disruptive non-random strategy: accuracy-gap removal disrupts 10-16× more (z=-157 vs -9.5), high-variance 11× more (z=-108), high-discrimination 1.7× more (z=-16). MH's ability conditioning filters out raw accuracy differences, isolating genuine differential functioning rather than generally informative items. DIF-discrimination overlap is only 22.1% (Jaccard=0.12) — DIF identifies \\\\\\\"items that changed across generations,\\\\\\\" not \\\\\\\"good items.\\\\\\\"\\\\n3. **DIF as diagnostic lens**: DIF identifies which items' functioning changes most across model generations → reveals which capability dimensions drive ranking shifts. Not \\\\\\\"find bad items and remove\\\\\\\" but \\\\\\\"understand what changed and why.\\\\\\\" Model-specific patterns: phi-3/llama-3 gain +700-850 ranks from DIF items, mistral merges lose -400 to -587. **Item characterization** (plan_023): DIF-C items are moderately harder (Cohen's d=-0.247) and higher-discrimination (d=0.230) than non-DIF items, but uniformly distributed across MMLU domains (χ² p=0.32). Key finding: difficulty × DIF direction interaction (ρ=-0.597) — hardest-decile DIF-C items are 88.7% focal-favoring (2024 models do better), easiest-decile are 96.2% reference-favoring (2023 models do better). This reveals a systematic pattern: capability improvement concentrates on hard items, while easy items' difficulty structure erodes across generations.\\\\n4. **Cross-benchmark validation (exploratory)**: On GSM8K (1,319 items × 6,702 models), DIF flags anti-correlate with GSM1K contamination gap (r=−0.478, p=0.005, N=33). **DEMOTED to exploratory** per Dream #6 three-tier consensus: N=33, CI wide [−0.71, −0.16], not strong enough for numbered contribution. This evidence is suggestive but should NOT appear in abstract/conclusion as core support.\\\\n- Evidence: plan_017 (z=-8 to -49σ, ρ=0.996-0.999), plan_020 (circularity ablation: DIF z=-9.5 vs acc-gap z=-157, disc z=-16, var z=-108), plan_023 (item characterization: difficulty ρ=-0.597, domain uniformity χ² p=0.32)\\\\n- Architecture: plan_017 is load-bearing (strong, full N=5,227). plan_020 circularity ablation is the second pillar. GSM1K correlation (plan_004 Step 3) is supplementary/exploratory only.\\\\n- Growing Pains互补: \\\\\\\"Growing Pains uses anchor items for efficient evaluation; our DIF diagnostic tells them which anchor items are degrading\\\\\\\"\\\\n- Caveats (→ limitations): N=33, Pearson CI upper bound |r|=0.155 (small effect possible), Spearman CI upper bound |ρ|=0.031 (near zero possible); item difficulty confound not fully excluded (DIF-C items harder for all models); contamination gap is proxy, not ground truth; replication with larger matched sample needed\\\\n\\\\n## Story Spine (v2 — no-pivot narrative)\\\\n\\\\n**Opening frame**: LLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability — when we say \\\\\\\"Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,\\\\\\\" we presume both scores reflect the same latent construct. But do they? Psychometrics has a mature tool for testing exactly this: Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis detects items whose difficulty changes across examinee groups after controlling for overall ability. We apply MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items) — the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.\\\\n\\\\n**Key narrative principle**: \\\\\\\"Instability ≠ invalidity.\\\\\\\" DIF flags items whose functioning changed across generations — this is a diagnostic signal, not a quality verdict. MMLU rankings are massively redundant (ρ≈0.997 after removing 31% of items), so instability doesn't threaten validity. But it reveals WHICH capability dimensions are shifting. **This framing must appear in the Introduction, not be deferred to Discussion.**\\\\n\\\\n**What we find** (4 contributions, focused):\\\\n\\\\n1. **31% of MMLU items are functionally unstable** across model generations (ETS C, |Δ_MH|≥1.5). Robust to threshold choice (49%/31%/18%/10% at 1.0/1.5/2.0/2.5), multiple testing (BH removes 3/3918 items), and architecture composition (99.98% decoder-only). Domain accuracy improvement explains ≤2.4pp of 31.3% C% (7.6%; pseudo R²=0.0007). The instability is predominantly item-specific, not a proxy for \\\\\\\"models got better.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n2. **Ground truth meta-critique**: Web-overlap contamination labels measure global exposure, not differential contamination. Enrichment≈1.0 is expected. Semi-synthetic injection confirms DIF detects differential contamination (ΔTPR=+0.508). Any item-level detector validated against web-overlap labels has this blind spot.\\\\n\\\\n3. **Semi-synthetic validation framework**: ΔTPR methodology with cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000). Portable across benchmarks (GSM8K ΔTPR=+0.818). Reusable for validating any item-level contamination detector.\\\\n\\\\n4. **DIF as diagnostic lens + actionable protocol**: DIF items carry cross-cohort signal (z=-8 to -49σ vs random) but are the LEAST disruptive non-random strategy (z=-9.5 vs accuracy-gap z=-157). DIF isolates genuine differential functioning, not just informative items. **Actionable contribution**: DIF audit protocol for benchmark maintainers (monitoring frequency, decision rules, response options).\\\\n\\\\n**Headline result**: \\\\\\\"Psychometric DIF analysis of 5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items reveals 31% temporal instability — robust across thresholds, not driven by capability improvement (≤7.6%), and not a validity threat (ρ≈0.997 ranking stability). The field's web-overlap ground truth cannot capture this differential signal; we provide a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000). DIF serves as a diagnostic lens for understanding what changed across model generations, with a concrete audit protocol for benchmark maintenance.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n## Scope Control (main body vs appendix)\\\\n\\\\n**MAIN BODY (4 claims + robustness)**:\\\\n- §1 Introduction (problem + \\\\\\\"instability ≠ invalidity\\\\\\\" framing upfront)\\\\n- §2 Related Work (45 papers, 5 clusters)\\\\n- §3 Method (MH-DIF primer, semi-synthetic framework, cross-subject matching)\\\\n- §4 Experiments: Claim 1 (temporal DIF + threshold + BH + decomposition + architecture), Claim 2 (enrichment + meta-critique), Claim 3 (semi-synthetic + cross-benchmark), Claim 4 (redundancy + circularity ablation + diagnostic lens + GSM1K validation)\\\\n- §5 Discussion + Limitations\\\\n- §6 Conclusion\\\\n\\\\n**APPENDIX (pruned from main body)**:\\\\n- Bayesian cumulative DIF (plan_007) — simulation-only, underdeveloped\\\\n- RL robustness (plan_002) — N=13, underpowered\\\\n- Ensemble fusion (plan_003a/b/c) — negative, not executed\\\\n- Bifactor equating pilot — below target fit\\\\n- Item generation pilot — below benchmarks\\\\n- Graceful degradation — clean but supplementary\\\\n- Item-Focused Trees — robustness check, supplementary\\\\n- ConStat/DVD comparisons — complementary baselines\\\\n\\\\n## Empirical Observation: Structural Constraints on DIF in LLM Benchmarks\\\\n\\\\nSystematic experimentation reveals that three structural improvements — matched-ability cohort design (plan_001b), IRT θ-conditioning (plan_012, r=0.9986), and bifactor pre-check (plan_014, within-subject C%=38.1%) — all fail to reduce temporal DIF baseline FPR below 31% while maintaining detection power. This is an empirical observation, not a formal impossibility result. The constraints arise from: (a) unidimensional IRT limitations in modeling 57-subject multidimensional ability, (b) statistical power requirements (N≥80, δ≥1.4), and (c) inability to separate ability confounding from genuine item-level changes. Higher-level methods (MIMIC, propensity-score matching, multidimensional IRT) may address individual constraints — future work.\\\\n\\\\n## Venue Fit\\\\n\\\\n- **EMNLP 2026**: Strong fit — NLP evaluation methodology is a dedicated area, reviewers expect empirical studies of benchmarks. \\\\\\\"Empirical, not algorithmic\\\\\\\" contribution type well-received. Risk: 8-page limit requires significant compression (current 10 pages). Mitigation: move structural analysis details to appendix.\\\\n\\\\n## Novelty Articulation (三层，显式化)\\\\n\\\\nMH-DIF (1959/1988) is applied without modification. **Explicitly acknowledge this.** Novelty is NOT algorithmic. Three layers of genuine contribution:\\\\n\\\\n1. **Empirical discovery at unprecedented scale**: First DIF analysis on 5,227×12,508 LLM response matrix. Finding: 31% temporal instability is robust across thresholds (10-49%), not explained by capability improvement (≤7.6%), and architecturally homogeneous (99.98% decoder-only). No prior work has characterized item-level stability of LLM benchmarks at this scale. The instability finding itself is new data about MMLU's measurement properties.\\\\n\\\\n2. **Ground truth meta-critique with field-wide implications**: The observation that web-overlap labels cannot validate item-level differential detectors is NOT obvious — the field routinely uses them. This affects the validity claims of Min-K%, ConStat, n-gram methods, and all papers using web-overlap as ground truth. First empirical demonstration (enrichment≈1.0 + semi-synthetic proof).\\\\n\\\\n3. **Semi-synthetic validation framework + cross-subject matching**: The ΔTPR methodology is a reusable infrastructure contribution. Cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000) is a novel practical innovation — the insight that within-subject matching contaminates the conditioning variable is subtle and general. Portable to any multi-domain benchmark (demonstrated on GSM8K).\\\\n\\\\n**Framing**: \\\\\\\"We bring mature psychometric tools to characterize an urgent ML evaluation problem. The contribution is the empirical findings, the validation methodology critique, and the reusable framework — not a new algorithm.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns (updated for pivot)\\\\n\\\\n1. **\\\\\\\"31% could just mean models got better / ability confound inflates C%\\\\\\\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_019 + plan_027). Item-level decomposition shows domain improvement explains ≤2.4pp of 31.3% C% (7.6%; pseudo R²=0.0007). Temporal cohort ability gap is negligible (d=-0.17, OVL=76.4%); 1:1 NN matching (d≈-0.01) yields C%=28.9%±0.1% vs full 31.3% — ability confound explains only 2.4pp. Contrast: Llama-2v3 (d=-0.99) drops from 61.3% to 28.3% under matching. Both converge to ~28-29% genuine item-level DIF.\\\\n\\\\n2. **\\\\\\\"Ground truth critique is obvious — of course web labels don't capture differential effects\\\\\\\"** — Not obvious: the field routinely uses these labels for item-level validation. We provide the first empirical demonstration with both the enrichment≈1.0 result and the semi-synthetic proof of concept.\\\\n\\\\n3. **\\\\\\\"Semi-synthetic injection is artificial\\\\\\\"** — We inject into real response matrices (not simulated), preserving real ability distributions and item characteristics. The mechanism (incorrect→correct flipping) is a simplification acknowledged in limitations.\\\\n\\\\n4. **\\\\\\\"DIF power is only sufficient for large effects\\\\\\\"** — Large effects are precisely what threaten benchmark validity most. Operational region: N≥80, δ≥1.4, TPR=0.68-0.80.\\\\n\\\\n5. **\\\\\\\"Only validated on MMLU\\\\\\\"** — Partially addressed: GSM8K replication (METABENCH 1,319 items × 6,702 models) confirms semi-synthetic framework portability (ΔTPR=+0.818) and temporal DIF pattern (C%=18.9%). **plan_028 in progress**: checking METABENCH for ARC/HellaSwag/WinoGrande/TruthfulQA response matrices to run multi-benchmark temporal DIF. Full generalization to diverse benchmarks remains future work if METABENCH lacks other benchmarks.\\\\n\\\\n6. **\\\\\\\"Higher methods (MIMIC, propensity-score) could solve the confounding\\\\\\\"** — Acknowledged as future work. We frame our three-attempt negative results as \\\\\\\"empirical observation\\\\\\\" that motivates these directions, not as impossibility.\\\\n\\\\n7. **\\\\\\\"ETS threshold is uncalibrated for LLM populations\\\\\\\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_018). C% at {1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5} = {48.9%, 31.3%, 18.3%, 10.2%}. Finding >15% holds at standard thresholds [1.0, 2.0]. At conservative 2.5, still 10.2% (1,278 items). Present as: \\\\\\\"finding is robust across standard thresholds; even at the most conservative cutoff, >10% of items show large DIF.\\\\\\\" Do NOT claim \\\\\\\">15% at all thresholds\\\\\\\" — be honest that 2.5 drops below.\\\\n\\\\n8. **\\\\\\\"Multiple testing: 12,508 simultaneous tests without correction\\\\\\\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_018). BH FDR correction at q=0.05 removes only 3/3,918 ETS C items (31.32%→31.30%). At higher thresholds, BH impact is zero. ETS dual requirement (effect size + significance) provides near-complete implicit protection. Random-split sanity check (C%=0.18%) confirms empirically.\\\\n\\\\n9. **\\\\\\\"Only uniform DIF — non-uniform DIF plausible for LLMs\\\\\\\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_021). LR-DIF on 9 subjects (1787 items): MH C-class 92.1% concordance with LR uniform DIF → MH detection robust. Non-uniform DIF: 38.7% at significance threshold, **24.8% at |β_interaction|≥0.5** (conservative, effect-size controlled). MH structurally underestimates instability. Caveat: LR used within-subject matching (vs MH's cross-benchmark), so 38.7% may be inflated; use 24.8% in paper. Framing: \\\\\\\"our 31% uniform-only estimate is conservative.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n10. **\\\\\\\"5,227 models are not independent — fine-tunes/merges inflate N and DIF significance\\\\\\\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_022). Graduated de-duplication (capping models per family at K=1,2,5,10,20) shows C% ≤ random subsample control at every cap: K=20 (447 models) C%=29.1% vs random 30.9%; K=5 (146 models) C%=11.7% vs random 16.9%; K=1 (33 families) C%=0.17% (underpowered, N<80). Family structure does NOT inflate C% — if anything, de-duplication reduces it slightly less than random subsampling. The 31.3% figure is robust to model non-independence.\\\\n\\\\n11. **\\\\\\\"Ranking disruption result is tautological\\\\\\\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_020). Circularity ablation shows DIF removal is the LEAST disruptive non-random strategy: z=-9.5 vs accuracy-gap z=-157, discrimination z=-16, variance z=-108. Despite 62.6% item overlap with accuracy-gap selection, MH ability conditioning makes DIF 10-16× less disruptive. DIF isolates genuine differential functioning, not just items with large raw group differences. Tautology refuted.\\\\n\\\\n## Dropped from Original Framing\\\\n\\\\n- **Ensemble (Claim 2 old)**: plan_003a/b/c never executed. Future work.\\\\n- **Regeneration (Claim 3 old)**: Bifactor killed by plan_014 pre-check. Dropped entirely.\\\\n- **RL robustness**: Theoretically motivated but N=13 insufficient. Limitations disclosure, not claim.\\\\n- **Detection as primary contribution**: Pivoted from \\\\\\\"we detect contamination\\\\\\\" to \\\\\\\"we characterize benchmark properties.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 20006, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01L27ir7ywE9CMtHXHS3NDtC","session_id":"9a39fd00-8805-41a7-a4c4-e92500e754cb","uuid":"3732b6b6-f4bb-46e6-805d-d1c216eb80b4","timestamp":"2026-05-21T11:55:36.599Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TLMQ6v4e6k9kga56VcK8wy","type":"tool_result","content":"1\texperiments:\n2\t  # === P0: Headline Results (must run) ===\n3\t\n4\t  - id: plan_001\n5\t    name: real_data_dif_mmlu\n6\t    claim: 1\n7\t    description: >\n8\t      Apply DIF analysis to METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,219 models × 12,508 items).\n9\t      Data characteristics: only 37% models classifiable by family (1,929); effective temporal\n10\t      span ~1.5 years (2023: N=1,253, 2024-H1: N=656); 15.4% items have negative discrimination.\n11\t      Design:\n12\t      (a) Primary cohort: temporal split (2023 vs 2024-H1, both N≥80 ✓)\n13\t      (b) Alternative cohort: accuracy-quintile split (ability-based grouping, uses all models)\n14\t      (c) Item filtering: run with and without negative-discrimination items (sensitivity analysis)\n15\t      Run ETS C DIF detection on all items under both cohort definitions.\n16\t      Proxy validation against Li et al. (EMNLP 2024) web-overlap contamination labels (9,229 items,\n17\t      73.8% coverage). IMPORTANT conceptual distinction: Li et al. labels are model-agnostic\n18\t      (item exists on web), while DIF detects cohort-differential contamination (item behaves\n19\t      differently across temporal cohorts). Low concordance is EXPECTED — DIF only catches items\n20\t      where contamination is differential across cohorts, not uniform. Primary validation metric:\n21\t      FPR analysis (DIF flags on Li-et-al-clean items) rather than TPR (which conflates uniform\n22\t      vs differential contamination). Unlabeled items (26.2%) reported separately as \"unknown\".\n23\t      plan_011 (semi-synthetic injection) provides controlled ground truth to complement this proxy.\n24\t      (d) Within-family confound control: run DIF within model families where both versions\n25\t          have N≥80. Confirmed feasible: Llama-2 (N=161) vs Llama-3 (N=308) ✓. Qwen-1.5 (N=67)\n26\t          below threshold — use as supplementary only. Identify additional qualifying pairs from\n27\t          METABENCH metadata. If DIF flags\n28\t          items that are NOT differentially contaminated within-family, the flag reflects capability\n29\t          gain, not contamination. Report: DIF flag overlap between cross-cohort and within-family\n30\t          analyses to disentangle contamination from capability improvement.\n31\t      (e) Domain-specificity analysis: correlate DIF flags with MMLU subject categories to check\n32\t          whether flags cluster in domains with known capability jumps vs known contamination.\n33\t      Report: DIF flags × cohort definition × item filtering — 4-way sensitivity matrix +\n34\t      within-family confound control + domain-specificity analysis.\n35\t    priority: P0\n36\t    gpu_budget_hours: 0\n37\t    deps: []\n38\t    registry_id: \"\"\n39\t\n40\t  - id: plan_002\n41\t    name: rl_robustness_validation\n42\t    claim: 1\n43\t    description: >\n44\t      Empirically validate DIF's RL-robustness claim. CRITICAL DISTINCTION:\n45\t      - \"Alignment RL\" (DPO/ORPO/KTO) = offline preference optimization, does NOT produce\n46\t        the NLL gap contraction that Wang et al. demonstrate. METABENCH has 477 such models.\n47\t      - \"Reasoning RL\" (GRPO/PPO online training) = the mechanism that conceals contamination\n48\t        signals. This is what Claim 1 targets. METABENCH has almost none of these.\n49\t      Design (Reviewer-validated):\n50\t      (a) SFT-only cohort from METABENCH as baseline\n51\t      (b) Alignment-RL cohort (DPO/ORPO, N=477) from METABENCH as mechanism control —\n52\t          DPO doesn't use PPO clipping, so no NLL gap contraction; if DIF works on both\n53\t          (a) and (b), robustness covers the full RL spectrum\n54\t      (c) Reasoning-RL models (DeepSeek-R1-7B/32B, Qwen2.5-RL, etc.) — run MMLU inference\n55\t          on 10-15 open-weight GRPO/PPO models. These are ADDED to the focal cohort\n56\t          (not a standalone group): DIF is group-level, so we verify that adding RL models\n57\t          to a cohort doesn't degrade detection. Separately, run traditional detectors\n58\t          (Min-K%, ConStat) on these 10-15 models individually to show per-model degradation.\n59\t      Two-level validation:\n60\t      - Group level: DIF flags same items regardless of cohort composition (a/b/c) → robust\n61\t      - Model level: traditional detectors degrade on (c) individuals → fragile\n62\t      This design works even with only 10-15 reasoning-RL models because DIF doesn't need\n63\t      N≥80 RL models — it needs N≥80 in the cohort total, and RL models are mixed in.\n64\t      Statistical rigor requirements:\n65\t      - Bootstrap 95% CIs on all AUROC comparisons (DIF vs ConStat vs Min-K%)\n66\t      - DeLong test or permutation test for pairwise AUROC differences\n67\t      - TOST equivalence testing for DIF retention ratio vs ConStat (not just null-result reasoning)\n68\t      (d) Partial exploitation power analysis: formally characterize DIF detection power as\n69\t          function of exploitation rate p ∈ [0.4, 1.0]. At p<1, contaminated model answers\n70\t          memorized items correctly with probability p, shrinking DIF effect size by ~p.\n71\t          Show detection boundary and ensemble coverage handoff point.\n72\t    priority: P0\n73\t    gpu_budget_hours: 100\n74\t    deps: [plan_001]\n75\t    registry_id: \"\"\n76\t\n77\t  - id: plan_003a\n78\t    name: constat_perturbation_eval\n79\t    claim: 2\n80\t    description: >\n81\t      Independent evaluation of ConStat-style perturbation testing on MMLU:\n82\t      (a) Generate semantically equivalent reformulations for 200+ MMLU items using LLM paraphrasing\n83\t      (b) Compare model performance on original vs reformulated items\n84\t      (c) Apply FDR-controlled detection (ConStat protocol)\n85\t      (d) Characterize detection power by effect size — expected strength on non-RL models, weakness on RL\n86\t      Even if DIF or DVD fails, ConStat standalone results have paper value as baseline comparison.\n87\t    priority: P0\n88\t    gpu_budget_hours: 80\n89\t    deps: [plan_001]\n90\t    registry_id: \"\"\n91\t\n92\t  - id: plan_003b\n93\t    name: dvd_generation_variance_eval\n94\t    claim: 2\n95\t    description: >\n96\t      Independent evaluation of DVD generation variance on MMLU:\n97\t      (a) Select 10-15 open-weight models with inference access\n98\t      (b) Generate 50 responses per item via temperature sampling (T=0.7-1.0)\n99\t      (c) Compute generation variance scores; detect bimodal distributions (contamination signal)\n100\t      (d) Characterize detection power for variant contamination vs verbatim contamination\n101\t      Standalone results provide variant-contamination detection baseline independent of DIF.\n102\t    priority: P0\n103\t    gpu_budget_hours: 70\n104\t    deps: [plan_001]\n105\t    registry_id: \"\"\n106\t\n107\t  - id: plan_003c\n108\t    name: ensemble_fusion\n109\t    claim: 2\n110\t    description: >\n111\t      Fuse DIF + ConStat + DVD into multi-signal ensemble:\n112\t      (a) Calibrate per-signal thresholds on subset of items with known contamination status\n113\t      (b) Evaluate 2-of-3 majority voting and weighted fusion strategies\n114\t      (c) Show ensemble coverage: DIF covers δ≥1.4, ConStat covers non-RL δ≥0.6, DVD covers variant\n115\t      (d) Precision analysis: ensemble FPR<0.10 across all configurations\n116\t      (e) Cross-validate fusion weights (5-fold); report uniform-weight baseline alongside optimized\n117\t      Depends on 003a and 003b completing; if one signal fails, degrade to 2-signal ensemble.\n118\t    priority: P0\n119\t    gpu_budget_hours: 0\n120\t    deps: [plan_001, plan_003a, plan_003b]\n121\t    registry_id: \"\"\n122\t\n123\t  # === P1: Strengthening Evidence ===\n124\t\n125\t  - id: plan_004\n126\t    name: cross_benchmark_gsm8k\n127\t    claim: 1\n128\t    description: >\n129\t      Replicate DIF analysis on GSM8K using GSM1K as ground truth for contamination.\n130\t      Collect per-item binary responses from 50+ models on GSM8K (via existing leaderboard data\n131\t      or inference). Run DIF across temporal cohorts. Correlate DIF flags with\n132\t      per-model GSM8K-GSM1K accuracy gap. Validates cross-benchmark generalizability\n133\t      and provides an independent ground truth beyond Li et al. proxy labels.\n134\t    priority: P1\n135\t    gpu_budget_hours: 100\n136\t    deps: [plan_001]\n137\t    registry_id: \"\"\n138\t\n139\t  - id: plan_005\n140\t    name: bifactor_irt_equating_pilot\n141\t    claim: 3\n142\t    description: >\n143\t      Pilot bifactor IRT equating on MMLU:\n144\t      NOTE: METABENCH preproc.rds contains CTT statistics (p-value, rpbis), NOT IRT parameters.\n145\t      True IRT params are in mirt model objects (crossvalidate.R). Must either extract from\n146\t      METABENCH mirt RDS files or refit from the 5219×12508 response matrix.\n147\t      (a) Fit bifactor model to METABENCH MMLU data (general factor + subject-specific factors)\n148\t      (b) Select anchor items (high-stability, no DIF across cohorts)\n149\t      (c) Calibrate replacement items via fixed-parameter calibration (Kim & Kolen 2019)\n150\t      (d) Demonstrate equated score equivalence: model rankings on original vs replaced benchmark\n151\t      should correlate at Spearman rho ≥ 0.95 after equating.\n152\t    priority: P1\n153\t    gpu_budget_hours: 50\n154\t    deps: [plan_001]\n155\t    registry_id: \"\"\n156\t\n157\t  - id: plan_006\n158\t    name: item_generation_calibration\n159\t    claim: 3\n160\t    description: >\n161\t      Generate replacement items for DIF-flagged MMLU questions:\n162\t      (a) Use LLM generation with construct constraints (same subject, same cognitive level)\n163\t      (b) Calibrate via student simulation (arXiv 2601.09953, r=0.75-0.82 for difficulty estimation)\n164\t      (c) Compare difficulty correlation: prompt-based targeting (rho=0.10-0.45 per arXiv 2602.06631)\n165\t      vs empirical calibration via student simulation\n166\t      (d) Validate on holdout model panel\n167\t    priority: P1\n168\t    gpu_budget_hours: 200\n169\t    deps: [plan_005]\n170\t    registry_id: \"\"\n171\t\n172\t  # === P2: Bonus / Extension ===\n173\t\n174\t  - id: plan_007\n175\t    name: cumulative_bayesian_dif\n176\t    claim: 1\n177\t    description: >\n178\t      Implement cumulative Bayesian DIF priors (Sinharay et al., 2006).\n179\t      Simulate 4 successive benchmark administrations. Show power improvement\n180\t      from accumulating evidence: individual administration power ~0.40\n181\t      → cumulative power ~0.70 after 3 administrations for medium effects (δ=1.0).\n182\t    priority: P2\n183\t    gpu_budget_hours: 0\n184\t    deps: [plan_001]\n185\t    registry_id: \"\"\n186\t\n187\t  - id: plan_008\n188\t    name: graceful_degradation_analysis\n189\t    claim: 3\n190\t    description: >\n191\t      Formal analysis of benchmark validity under item replacement:\n192\t      (a) Define validity metrics (ranking stability, score reliability, construct coverage)\n193\t      (b) Simulate progressive replacement (5%, 10%, 20%, 50% items replaced)\n194\t      (c) Establish theoretical bounds on validity degradation as function of replacement rate\n195\t      (d) Empirical validation using METABENCH data\n196\t    priority: P2\n197\t    gpu_budget_hours: 0\n198\t    deps: [plan_005, plan_006]\n199\t    registry_id: \"\"\n200\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Uf4n8svxJGuXqkC5FaAwvF","session_id":"9a39fd00-8805-41a7-a4c4-e92500e754cb","uuid":"f7b5e4ad-cef9-4852-a24e-ddd7d2226021","timestamp":"2026-05-21T11:55:36.464Z"}
